# Negative control (the forge half): the devbox image with the forge directory on PATH.
#
# Added after measuring which assertions the first three controls actually
# reach. Two did not have a control at all —
# `test_the_name_resolves_to_the_wrapper[gh]` and `[glab]` — and two more
# (`test_the_real_binary_is_off_path`) only ever went red through the
# `test -x` guard raising in the no-forge image, never through the comparison
# they are written around. Four forge assertions, unfalsified, in the change whose
# entire purpose was to stop assertions being unfalsified.
#
# The defect it reproduces is real and small: someone puts
# /usr/local/lib/istota_forge on PATH, reasoning that the binaries are there
# and it is convenient. `gh` then resolves to the real CLI ahead of the wrapper
# at /usr/local/bin, so the deny policy is never consulted and the token is
# never injected — the same outcome as control 3, reached by the route that is
# actually likely rather than by overwriting a file.
#
# This is why the off-PATH placement is worth asserting rather than assuming:
# it is one environment variable away from being undone, and nothing about
# reading `docker/devbox/Dockerfile` tells you whether the tests would notice.
#
# Usage:
#
#   docker build -f docker/test/Dockerfile.devbox-forge-dir-on-path \
#       --build-arg BASE=<the devbox tag conftest built> \
#       -t istota-test/devbox-forge-dir-on-path:control docker/test
ARG BASE
FROM ${BASE}

# No file changes at all — the difference is entirely the search order, which
# is what makes this the cleanest of the four: nothing about the image's
# contents differs from the product.
ENV PATH="/usr/local/lib/istota_forge:${PATH}"
